        \ call returns `true`, then I immediately return `true` as well.\\n\\nIf the\
        \ value is an array, I'll need to iterate through each element of the array\
        \ and recursively call `filterHasNow` on each element. Again, if any recursive\
        \ call returns `true`, I'll bubble that up.\\n\\nIf I reach the end of the\
        \ iteration without finding `$NOW` at any level, then I return `false`.\\\
        n\\nThe TypeScript code snippet looks solid for handling this recursive traversal,\
        \ and it seems to have taken the key structures that the Directus `Filter`\
        \ type can present in to account. `typeof value === 'object'` correctly handles\
        \ both arrays and objects within the filter. This should provide the necessary\
        \ coverage.\\n\\n\\n\""
      - '"**My Analysis of `authenticateConnection`**\n\nOkay, let''s break down this
        `authenticateConnection` function. My initial thought is it''s handling the
        initial authentication of a WebSocket connection. The input is a `message`
        which likely contains authentication credentials, and potentially `accountabilityOverrides`
        for specific cases. The output is a `Promise` resolving to an `AuthenticationState`,
        which will contain the `accountability` object, `expires_at` timestamp, and
        a potential `refresh_token` if one was used.\n\nFirst, I need to extract the
        `access_token` and `refresh_token` from the incoming `message`. The core authentication
        logic hinges on these. There''s also likely a `mode` parameter involved, though
        its specific usage isn''t immediately obvious, but it''s used with `BasicAuthMessage`.\n\nThe
        tests show the crucial part: if `getAccountabilityForToken` throws an error
        related to an expired token, I need to reject with a `WebSocketError` containing
        the message \"Authentication failed.\". That''s straightforward error handling.
        The other test is a good sign that the `emitter.emitFilter` call is working,
        and correctly modifying the accountability object if configured. It appears
        to be short-circuiting, meaning it can bypass any other authentication process.\n\nNow,
        let''s look at the refresh token flow. If a `refresh_token` is present, it
        seems I need to use the `AuthenticationService`. This likely means I''ll create
        an instance of `AuthenticationService`, and call `authService.refresh(refresh_token)`.
        That will hopefully return a new `accessToken` and `refreshToken`. The success
        test confirms this. The output structure includes `accountability`, `expires_at`,
        and a transformed `refresh_token`. The `refresh_token` changes from `refresh_token`
        to `refresh`.  I need to remember this for consistency.\n\nIf no `refresh_token`
        or `access_token` are provided, I need to use the `createDefaultAccountability()`
        function.\n\nOnce I have an `access_token`, I need to use `getAccountabilityForToken`
        to get the base `accountability`.  I need to be mindful of catching errors
        from this call and throwing a `WebSocketError` with the \"Authentication failed.\"
        message as expected. Then, I''ll retrieve the `expires_at` using `getExpiresAtForToken`.\n\nThere''s
        a key step after that: applying any `accountabilityOverrides`. This allows
        for adjusting the `accountability` object based on other factors, perhaps
        from the incoming message or context. This is what the `accountabilityOverrides`
        parameter is for.\n\nFinally, the filter: I''ll need to call `emitter.emitFilter(''authenticate'',
        accountability, message, { database, schema })`. This lets me customize the
        `accountability` object even further. It appears this filter is crucial for
        the overall flow.\n\nLooking at the example code, it all makes sense.  The
        code clearly indicates the use of `AuthenticationService`, `getDatabase`,
        `getSchema`, and also correctly utilizes the `try...catch` block for error
        handling.\n\nThe return is a neatly constructed object with `accountability`,
        `expires_at`, and the `refresh_token` if applicable. It''s a clean, efficient
        approach to authenticating WebSocket connections.\n\n\n"'
